# 12. Vocabulary flashcards — 25 core terms (page carousel)

These 25 terms are the highest-frequency vocabulary across all seven units, sampled from the 350-term master list. The on-page carousel shows 25 at a time; clicking through to a unit page unlocks the remaining 325.

### The 25 cards (front / back)

| **#** | **Term (front)** | **Unit** | **Definition (back)** |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | Absolute Location | Unit 1 | A fixed coordinate, usually latitude and longitude. Example: Tokyo = 35.68° N, 139.69° E. |
| 2 | Relative Location | Unit 1 | A location described in relation to another place. Example: “North of the river.” |
| 3 | Choropleth Map | Unit 1 | A thematic map that uses color or shading within areas to display statistical data. |
| 4 | Diffusion | Unit 1 | The spread of an idea, culture, or innovation across space over time. |
| 5 | Scale of Analysis | Unit 1 | The level at which geographic data is studied — local, regional, national, or global. |
| 6 | Demographic Transition Model | Unit 2 | A 5-stage model of population change from high birth + death to low birth + death. |
| 7 | Dependency Ratio | Unit 2 | The ratio of non-working (young + old) to working-age population × 100. |
| 8 | Push Factor | Unit 2 | A negative condition that drives people to leave a place. |
| 9 | Pull Factor | Unit 2 | A positive condition that attracts people to a new place. |
| 10 | Cultural Landscape | Unit 3 | The visible human imprint on the environment — buildings, fields, signs, religious structures. |
| 11 | Universalizing Religion | Unit 3 | A religion that actively seeks converts across cultures. Christianity, Islam, Buddhism. |
| 12 | Cultural Hearth | Unit 3 | An area where a cultural trait originated and from which it diffused. |
| 13 | Sovereignty | Unit 4 | A state's recognized right to govern its territory without external interference. |
| 14 | Gerrymandering | Unit 4 | Manipulating electoral boundaries to favor a political party through packing or cracking. |
| 15 | Devolution | Unit 4 | The transfer of political power from a central government to regional governments. |
| 16 | Supranational Organization | Unit 4 | Multiple states cooperating on shared issues while keeping sovereignty. EU, UN, NATO. |
| 17 | Green Revolution | Unit 5 | Mid-20th-century shift to high-yield seeds, chemical fertilizers, and irrigation — especially in South Asia. |
| 18 | Von Thünen Model | Unit 5 | A rings model of agricultural land use around a central market, ordered by perishability and transport cost. |
| 19 | Subsistence Agriculture | Unit 5 | Farming primarily to feed one's own family or community, not for market sale. |
| 20 | Primate City | Unit 6 | The largest city in a country, more than twice the size of the second-largest. |
| 21 | Central-Place Theory | Unit 6 | A model of city size and spacing based on the range of goods and services they offer. |
| 22 | Gentrification | Unit 6 | Wealthier residents moving into and renovating urban neighborhoods, often displacing poorer residents. |
| 23 | Weber's Least-Cost Theory | Unit 7 | A model locating industry based on transport cost, labor cost, and agglomeration benefits. |
| 24 | Wallerstein's World Systems | Unit 7 | Divides the world into core, semi-periphery, and periphery based on economic role. |
| 25 | Human Development Index (HDI) | Unit 7 | A composite of life expectancy, education, and GNI per capita. A broader measure than GDP alone. |

